<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prayagraj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Allahabad, Prayag</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prayagraj, called Allahabad from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> until 2018, is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, a sacred bathing place for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. It hosts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbh-mela">Kumbh Mela</a>, one of the largest religious gatherings in the world. Its fort holds the Ashokan pillar bearing the Allahabad inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a>, and it was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prayagraj.jpg" alt="Prayagraj" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dananuj · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allahabad_Montage_II_Dec_2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Prayagraj lies in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, at the Triveni Sangam, the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and the mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Sarasvati</a>, which according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> legend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> up from underground. The place was known in ancient times as Prayāga, meaning place of a sacrifice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, since the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> was believed to have performed the first sacrifice there.</p><p>The name Prayag alone is still used for the city. Geographically it is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Ganga-Yamuna Doab</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>. The Prayag Kumbh Mela is held at the Sangam every 12 years, and the ashes of several national leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> in 1948, have been immersed there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest mention of Prayāga as a pilgrimage is in the Rigveda Pariśiṣṭa, a supplement of about 1200 to 1000 BCE. The Pali canon records the Buddha saying that bathing there cannot wash away cruel deeds, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> describes a bathing pilgrimage at Prayag as atonement for past wrongs. The city is also sacred to Jains, who believe their first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a>, Rishabhanatha, attained kevala jnana there.</p><p>Some recent studies trace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta dynasty</a> to the Prayaga region, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allahabad-pillar-inscription">Allahabad Pillar Inscription</a> indicates its early centre of power. In 644 the Chinese traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> described Prayaga as a great city with hundreds of temples, two Buddhist institutions to its south, and a Grand Place of Almsgiving where wealthy people and kings gave alms.</p><p>Abul Fazal records in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari">Ain-i-Akbari</a> that on 13 November 1583 the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> reached the spot where the rivers meet, laid the foundations of a city and planned four forts. It was called Ilahabas or Ilahabad, later Allahabad. British troops were first stationed in the fort in 1765 under the Treaty of Allahabad, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>, the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> acquired the city in 1801.</p><p>In 1857 Maulvi Liaquat Ali raised the banner of revolt there, and Allahabad was the scene of a massacre of European troops. It became the capital of the North-Western Provinces in 1858, and for a day the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and was capital of the United Provinces from 1902 to 1920. The Civil Lines quarter, built in 1857, was the largest town-planning project in India before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city is the judicial capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, with the Allahabad High Court, and the administrative headquarters of Prayagraj district. Hindi is the most widely spoken language. Its landmarks include the Triveni Sangam, Allahabad University, All Saints Cathedral and Anand Bhavan, and it has buildings in Indo-Islamic and Indo-Saracenic styles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbh-mela">Kumbh Mela</a> held there is among the largest peaceful gatherings in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prayagraj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Allahabad</b>, Varanasi and Ayodhya were important pilgrimage sites for followers of Hinduism and other Dharmic religions, and the town of Bahraich was revered by some Muslims.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 November 1858 Lord Canning, the first viceroy, read Queen Victoria’s proclamation at <b>Allahabad</b>, promising religious toleration, equal access to office and respect for the rights of the Indian princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors Trilochanapala and Yashahpala ruled a small territory around Kannauj and <b>Allahabad</b>, and Yashahpala’s inscription of 1036 is the last record of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…authority contracted to the Punjab, Gandhara and Kashmir, while Mathura and the Ganges valley passed to local powers such as the Nagas and later to the Gupta Empire; the <b>Allahabad</b> pillar inscription of Samudragupta mentions the homage of a 'Daivaputra-Shahi-Shahanushahi', generally taken to be a late Kushan ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the mid-fourth century the <b>Allahabad</b> pillar inscription of Samudragupta lists Vishnugopa of Kanchi among the southern kings defeated during the Gupta ruler's southern campaign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…book claimed that it had been a Hindu temple, but India's Supreme Court dismissed his petition in 2000, the <b>Allahabad</b> High Court dismissed a similar one in 2005, and in 2017 the Archaeological Survey of India declared that there was no evidence it had ever housed a temple.</blockquote><a href="/work/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
